(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2809042000-hermes-profile-fitness-coscientist-iteration-01")
  (:adr/title "hermes agent bots: a registry that covers the fleet, and four measurements I had to correct before reporting")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-04")
  (:adr/related (vector "ADR-2608313500" "ADR-2607173100" "ADR-2608124800" "ADR-2607132300"
                "adr-2809041200-doubt-rules-not-grounded-in-properties"
                "adr-2809041800-ayatori-coscientist-iteration-01-round-trips"))
  (:adr/context
  "The hermes agent bot fleet had no registry that covered it. Its live cron definitions sit in ~/.hermes/profiles/<p>/cron/jobs.json, are terminal-local, and are in no repo; scripts/hermes-cron-jobs/ exists precisely to close that, and its README states the purpose as making a new terminal's rebuild procedural.

Measured 2026-09-04: 97 profiles carried cron jobs and 21 were in the ledger. A rebuild from it would have restored 21% of 154 jobs, and nothing said so.

The ledger's own --check reports this correctly -- it exits 1 with 'STALE: ledger differs from live cron definitions'. Nothing ran it. It appeared in no detector registry, no cron job and no gate; the only two references in the whole tree were its own README and its own output file.

And it recorded generated_at=\"regenerated-on-run\" -- a literal, not a timestamp. That was deliberate: --check compared the file byte for byte, so a real timestamp would have fired STALE on every run. It bought a working check at the price of a ledger nobody could date, which is the same failure this workspace keeps finding: a registry that cannot say how old it is reads exactly like a current one.

Adjacent, and unregistered in a different way: 19 scripts/hermes-* directories hold 67 .py and 7 .sh against 9 .cljs, which is outside the runtime priority CLAUDE.md states. That is recorded here and not acted on -- a 74-file migration is not this iteration's work.")
  (:adr/decision
  "1. THE LEDGER COVERS THE FLEET, AND CAN BE DATED. export_cron.py now writes a real UTC timestamp, and --check normalises that one field before comparing, so both hold at once: it stays quiet when the definitions match (verified idempotent one second apart) and the file says when it was written. Regenerated: 21 -> 97 profiles, 154 jobs, coverage 21.4% -> 100%. The output was scanned for credential shapes before landing; the single Bearer match is the placeholder name RESEND_EXECUTOR_TOKEN, not a value.

2. SOMETHING KEEPS LOOKING. scripts/verify-hermes-profile-fitness.cljs, registered in manifest/orgs-detectors.edn on the 6h tick. It counts and rates nothing: ledger coverage, jobs whose :script or :workdir resolves nowhere, the primary-model split, and failure_streak. It cannot be a fleet gate for the reason ADR-2608124800 gives -- its whole input is ~/.hermes on the operator workstation, which exists in no repo tree and on no node. It reads no credential; job prompts are counted, never printed. All three exits checked: 0 (--self-test, 14 assertions), 1 (live fleet, 4 findings), 2 (missing profiles root, refuses rather than reporting a healthy fleet from an absent one).

3. THE MODEL LAYER IS MEASURED WHERE IT DECIDES, AND THE SPLIT IS REPORTED RATHER THAN JUDGED. Primary model.default across 155 profiles: 119 on z-ai/glm-5.3-flash (ADR-2608313500's measured choice), 25 on murakumo-main (ADR-2607173100's fleet alias), 11 other or unreadable. The decision reached 77% of the fleet. What remains is not a violation but a split between two accepted ADRs that do not reference each other: 2608313500 names a concrete id after measuring it N=24, 2607173100 says consumers should name the alias so a model swap is one KV PUT rather than a sweep. A profile on the alias is following a rule, not breaking one, and 2608313500 mentions murakumo-main only as the subject of a provider bug -- it never mentions the alias rule. Picking a side is an owner decision with an ADR behind it, not something a counter should do quietly.")
  (:adr/consequences
  "Standing findings after the fixes, from the live fleet: 6 dangling scripts (all in the `otent` profile, pointing at a removed worktree), 19 jobs naming a :workdir that does not exist across 14 directories (mostly ~/.gftd/worktrees/*, cleaned up and never re-registered), the model split above, and 19 jobs carrying failure_streak > 0. These are fleet data, not tooling, and the detector's job is now to keep them visible rather than to fix them.

FOUR MEASUREMENTS HAD TO BE CORRECTED BEFORE THEY WERE REPORTED, and all four were the tool being wrong rather than the fleet. (a) :script is a bare filename and a job with no :workdir runs it from its own profile directory; resolving against the process cwd reported 79 of 83 missing when the real number is 6. (b) Counting a profile as scheduled because cron/jobs.json EXISTS raised a coverage finding against `aozora`, whose jobs list is empty and which export_cron.py rightly omits -- 100% read as 99%. (c) Warning that 61 of 154 jobs declared no :model was noise about the wrong layer: every one inherits a profile default that exists. (d) A first pass at the profile default mixed model.default with fallback_providers[].model and produced a distribution in which the decided primary appeared ZERO times; the correct reading is 119. Each of (a) through (d) would have been an alarming, actionable, false report. Corrections (a), (b) and (c) are pinned as assertions in both directions; (d) is pinned by a reader test that feeds both shapes at once.

That rate is the finding worth carrying forward. A fitness function is a measurement instrument, and an instrument that is wrong in the direction of 'broken' is worse than silence, because silence does not get acted on. The Co-Scientist loop's value here was not the hypotheses it generated -- it was that every number had to survive being asked 'can this tool see what it claims to be counting?' before it left the terminal.

Not done, and stated rather than implied: the 19 missing workdirs and 6 dangling scripts are not repaired, because deciding whether a bot is still wanted is not a counting question. The .py/.sh population is recorded and untouched. And the detector reads this workstation only -- there is no second terminal to compare against, so 'the fleet' throughout means the fleet on this machine.")))
